      "proposal": {
        "hypothesis": "For each contributing light, the current code normalizes L=d/r and then normalizes L−V. Since r>0, L−V and d−rV have the same direction; compute N·L as dot(N,d)/r and N·H as dot(N,d−rV)/|d−rV| instead. This removes component-wise normalization of both vectors while preserving the BRDF algebraically; the reordered floating-point operations require controller validation. The catalog reports three distinct valid programs (no repeats): baseline 0.11495 s, direct half-vector dot 0.10920 s, packed-light cache 0.11101 s, and their measured combination 0.10518 s. This contrasts with the cache and previous half-vector formulations by testing a scale-invariant half-vector representation, not by assuming an unmeasured gain.",
        "transformations": {
          "retained": [
            "In ShadeTileC, compute NdotL from the unnormalized light displacement multiplied by the reciprocal light distance, rather than materializing normalized L.",
            "Form the half-vector direction from displacement minus Vneg times light distance, then compute NdotH from its dot product and reciprocal length; leave powf, attenuation, light colors, and all other shading operations unchanged."
